To create a React component using hooks to convert between different temperature units, follow these steps:

1. Create a new React component file (e.g., `TemperatureConverter.js`) and import the necessary hooks:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';
```

2. Define a state variable for the input temperature and another state variable for the selected temperature unit:

```javascript
const [inputTemp, setInputTemp] = useState('');
const [selectedUnit, setSelectedUnit] = useState('celsius');
```

3. Create a function to convert the input temperature to the selected temperature unit:

```javascript
function convertTemp(temp, unit) {
  if (unit === 'celsius') {
    return temp * 9 / 5 + 32;
  } else if (unit === 'fahrenheit') {
    return (temp - 32) * 5 / 9;
  } else {
    return temp;
  }
}
```

4. Create a form with an input field for the input temperature and a dropdown menu for selecting the output temperature unit:

```javascript
return (
  <div>
    <h2>Temperature Converter</h2>
    <form>
      <label htmlFor="input-temp">Enter a temperature:</label>
      <input type="number" id="input-temp" value={inputTemp} onChange={(e) => setInputTemp(e.target.value)} />
      <label htmlFor="output-unit">Select an output unit:</label>
      <select id="output-unit" value={selectedUnit} onChange={(e) => setSelectedUnit(e.target.value)}>
        <option value="celsius">Celsius</option>
        <option value="fahrenheit">Fahrenheit</option>
      </select>
      <button onClick={() => setOutputTemp(convertTemp(inputTemp, selectedUnit))}>Convert</button>
      <p>Result: {outputTemp}</p>
    </form>
  </div>
);
```

5. Export the component:

```javascript
export default TemperatureConverter;
```

